{"st":"NJ","name":"New Jersey","seats":[{"census":1789,"seats":4},{"census":1790,"seats":5},{"census":1800,"seats":6},{"census":1810,"seats":6},{"census":1820,"seats":6},{"census":1830,"seats":6},{"census":1840,"seats":5},{"census":1850,"seats":5},{"census":1860,"seats":5},{"census":1870,"seats":7},{"census":1880,"seats":7},{"census":1890,"seats":8},{"census":1900,"seats":10},{"census":1910,"seats":12},{"census":1920,"seats":12},{"census":1930,"seats":14},{"census":1940,"seats":14},{"census":1950,"seats":14},{"census":1960,"seats":15},{"census":1970,"seats":15},{"census":1980,"seats":14},{"census":1990,"seats":13},{"census":2000,"seats":13},{"census":2010,"seats":12},{"census":2020,"seats":12}],"events":[{"congress":1,"year":1787,"kind":"statehood","title":"Ratified the Constitution","text":"New Jersey ratified the Constitution on December 18, 1787, the 3rd state to do so. The Constitution gave it 4 seats in the House until the first census.","sources":["https://www.everycrsreport.com/reports/R47747.html","https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":1,"year":1788,"kind":"at-large","title":"Statewide (general ticket) elections","text":"In the 1st through 5th Congresses (1789–1799), New Jersey elected all of its representatives statewide on a general ticket instead of by district.","sources":["https://github.com/unitedstates/congress-legislators","https://history.house.gov/Institution/Apportionment/Apportionment/"]},{"congress":3,"year":1792,"kind":"census","title":"1790 census apportionment","text":"After the 1790 census, New Jersey's House delegation grew from 4 to 5 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":7,"year":1800,"kind":"at-large","title":"Statewide (general ticket) elections","text":"In the 7th through 12th Congresses (1801–1813), New Jersey elected all of its representatives statewide on a general ticket instead of by district.","sources":["https://github.com/unitedstates/congress-legislators","https://history.house.gov/Institution/Apportionment/Apportionment/"]},{"congress":8,"year":1802,"kind":"census","title":"1800 census apportionment","text":"After the 1800 census, New Jersey's House delegation grew from 5 to 6 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":13,"year":1812,"kind":"census","title":"1810 census apportionment","text":"After the 1810 census, New Jersey kept 6 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":14,"year":1814,"kind":"at-large","title":"Statewide (general ticket) elections","text":"In the 14th through 27th Congresses (1815–1843), New Jersey elected all of its representatives statewide on a general ticket instead of by district.","sources":["https://github.com/unitedstates/congress-legislators","https://history.house.gov/Institution/Apportionment/Apportionment/"]},{"congress":18,"year":1822,"kind":"census","title":"1820 census apportionment","text":"After the 1820 census, New Jersey kept 6 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":23,"year":1832,"kind":"census","title":"1830 census apportionment","text":"After the 1830 census, New Jersey kept 6 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":28,"year":1842,"kind":"census","title":"1840 census apportionment","text":"After the 1840 census, New Jersey's House delegation shrank from 6 to 5 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":33,"year":1852,"kind":"census","title":"1850 census apportionment","text":"After the 1850 census, New Jersey kept 5 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":38,"year":1862,"kind":"census","title":"1860 census apportionment","text":"After the 1860 census, New Jersey kept 5 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":43,"year":1872,"kind":"census","title":"1870 census apportionment","text":"After the 1870 census, New Jersey's House delegation grew from 5 to 7 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":48,"year":1882,"kind":"census","title":"1880 census apportionment","text":"After the 1880 census, New Jersey kept 7 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":53,"year":1892,"kind":"census","title":"1890 census apportionment","text":"After the 1890 census, New Jersey's House delegation grew from 7 to 8 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":58,"year":1902,"kind":"census","title":"1900 census apportionment","text":"After the 1900 census, New Jersey's House delegation grew from 8 to 10 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":63,"year":1912,"kind":"census","title":"1910 census apportionment","text":"After the 1910 census, New Jersey's House delegation grew from 10 to 12 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":73,"year":1932,"kind":"census","title":"1930 census apportionment","text":"After the 1930 census, New Jersey's House delegation grew from 12 to 14 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":78,"year":1942,"kind":"census","title":"1940 census apportionment","text":"After the 1940 census, New Jersey kept 14 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":83,"year":1952,"kind":"census","title":"1950 census apportionment","text":"After the 1950 census, New Jersey kept 14 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":88,"year":1962,"kind":"census","title":"1960 census apportionment","text":"After the 1960 census, New Jersey's House delegation grew from 14 to 15 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":93,"year":1972,"kind":"census","title":"1970 census apportionment","text":"After the 1970 census, New Jersey kept 15 seats in the House. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":98,"year":1982,"kind":"census","title":"1980 census apportionment","text":"After the 1980 census, New Jersey's House delegation shrank from 15 to 14 seats. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":98,"year":1983,"kind":"court","title":"Karcher v. Daggett","text":"The Supreme Court struck down New Jersey's congressional map even though its largest population difference was under 1%, because the state had not shown the differences served a legitimate goal.","sources":["https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/462/725"]},{"congress":103,"year":1992,"kind":"census","title":"1990 census apportionment","text":"After the 1990 census, New Jersey's House delegation shrank from 14 to 13 seats. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":104,"year":1995,"kind":"other","title":"Redistricting commission made permanent","text":"A constitutional amendment effective December 7, 1995, created a 13-member commission to redraw congressional districts each decade. Legislative leaders and party chairs appoint 12 members, who pick an independent 13th member, or the state Supreme Court chooses one if they deadlock.","sources":["https://redistricting.lls.edu/state/new-jersey/"],"mapChange":false},{"congress":108,"year":2002,"kind":"census","title":"2000 census apportionment","text":"After the 2000 census, New Jersey kept 13 seats in the House. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":113,"year":2011,"kind":"other","title":"Map redrawn for 12 seats","text":"New Jersey dropped from 13 seats to 12. On December 23, 2011, the commission's independent chair cast the deciding vote for one of the two party delegations' maps, which merged two northern districts.","sources":["https://redistricting.lls.edu/state/new-jersey/","https://wnyc.org/story/177434-blog-tiebreaker-pick-gop-map-nj-house-seats/"]},{"congress":113,"year":2012,"kind":"census","title":"2010 census apportionment","text":"After the 2010 census, New Jersey's House delegation shrank from 13 to 12 seats. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/decennial/2020/data/apportionment/apportionment.csv","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":118,"year":2021,"kind":"other","title":"Commission adopts current map","text":"The state Supreme Court picked the commission's 13th member after the others could not agree. The commission adopted a map on December 22, 2021, and the state Supreme Court dismissed a challenge to it on February 3, 2022.","sources":["https://redistricting.lls.edu/state/new-jersey/"]},{"congress":118,"year":2022,"kind":"census","title":"2020 census apportionment","text":"After the 2020 census, New Jersey kept 12 seats in the House. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/decennial/2020/data/apportionment/apportionment.csv","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]}],"compactness":{"c001":{"NJ-0":0.121},"c002":{"NJ-0":0.121},"c003":{"NJ-0":0.121},"c004":{"NJ-0":0.121},"c005":{"NJ-0":0.121},"c006":{"NJ-1":0.233,"NJ-2":0.385,"NJ-3":0.486,"NJ-4":0.194,"NJ-5":0.144},"c007":{"NJ-0":0.122},"c008":{"NJ-0":0.122},"c009":{"NJ-0":0.122},"c010":{"NJ-0":0.122},"c011":{"NJ-0":0.122},"c012":{"NJ-0":0.122},"c013":{"NJ-1":0.406,"NJ-2":0.178,"NJ-3":0.158},"c014":{"NJ-0":0.122},"c015":{"NJ-0":0.122},"c016":{"NJ-0":0.122},"c017":{"NJ-0":0.122},"c018":{"NJ-0":0.122},"c019":{"NJ-0":0.122},"c020":{"NJ-0":0.122},"c021":{"NJ-0":0.122},"c022":{"NJ-0":0.122},"c023":{"NJ-0":0.122},"c024":{"NJ-0":0.122},"c025":{"NJ-0":0.122},"c026":{"NJ-0":0.122},"c027":{"NJ-0":0.122},"c028":{"NJ-1":0.144,"NJ-2":0.18,"NJ-3":0.342,"NJ-4":0.303,"NJ-5":0.335},"c029":{"NJ-1":0.144,"NJ-2":0.197,"NJ-3":0.441,"NJ-4":0.385,"NJ-5":0.336},"c030":{"NJ-1":0.144,"NJ-2":0.181,"NJ-3":0.342,"NJ-4":0.308,"NJ-5":0.336},"c031":{"NJ-1":0.144,"NJ-2":0.181,"NJ-3":0.342,"NJ-4":0.308,"NJ-5":0.336},"c032":{"NJ-1":0.144,"NJ-2":0.181,"NJ-3":0.342,"NJ-4":0.308,"NJ-5":0.336},"c033":{"NJ-1":0.144,"NJ-2":0.181,"NJ-3":0.297,"NJ-4":0.475,"NJ-5":0.34},"c034":{"NJ-1":0.144,"NJ-2":0.181,"NJ-3":0.297,"NJ-4":0.475,"NJ-5":